    <title>Practice Worksheet</title>




<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Use the table to find each of the following.
</p>

<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>-5</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>10</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>g(t)</m></cell>
<cell><m>9</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>9</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>

</introduction>

<task>
<statement>
<p>
<m>g(0)</m>
</p>
</statement>
</task>

<task>
<statement>
<p>
<m>g(5)</m>
</p>
</statement>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
The value(s) of <m>t</m> for which <m>g(t)=9</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
From the table, <m>g(0)=-1</m>, <m>g(5)=9</m>, and lastly <m>t=-5</m> and <m>t=5</m> both satisfy <m>g(t)=9</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

</exercise>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
A function <m>f(x)</m> is given numerically below. Compute the following:
</p>


<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
<cell><m>10</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>3.7</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>4.2</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>


</introduction>

<task>
<statement>
<p>
<m>f(4)</m>
</p>
</statement>
</task>

<task>
<statement>
<p>
<m>f(2)</m> 
</p>
</statement>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>f(8)</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
From the table, <m>f(4)=1.5</m>, <m>f(2)=3.7</m>, and <m>f(8)=1.5</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

</exercise>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Let <m>f(x)</m> be the function in the previous problem.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Find all inputs <m>x</m> for which <m>f(x)=1.5</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
When <m>f(x)=1.5</m> (can think of this as <m>y</m>, the second row in the table), then <m>x</m> could be <m>0</m>, <m>4</m>, and <m>8</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Find all inputs <m>x</m> for which <m>f(x)=3.7</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
When <m>f(x)=3.7</m>, then <m>x</m> could only be <m>2</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Guess a formula for the function <m>g(x)</m> given numerically in the table below.
</p>

<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>-8</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
<cell><m>27</m></cell>
<cell><m>64</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>

</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Here, <m>g(x)=x^3</m>. Convince yourself that this formula works!
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Guess a formula for the function <m>h(t)</m> given numerically in the table below. Fill in the missing values.
</p>

<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>h(t)</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-4</m></cell>
<cell><m>-6</m></cell>
<cell><m>-8</m></cell>
<cell/>
<cell/>
</row>
</tabular>

</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Notice that the inputs <m>t</m> go up by <m>1</m> each time. And, the outputs <m>h(t)</m> go down by <m>2</m> each time. Or, you can think of the outputs as being the inputs multiplied by <m>-2</m>.

Therefore, <m>h(t)=-2t</m>. Thus <m>h(5)=-10</m> and <m>h(6)=-12</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Here is the data for the world population
<fn><url href="https://www.worldometers.info/world-population/world-population-by-year/">https://www.worldometers.info/world-population/world-population-by-year/</url>, accessed: 7/8/20</fn> between 2010 and 2018, in billions:
</p>

<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell>Year</cell>
<cell>2010</cell>
<cell>2011</cell>
<cell>2012</cell>
<cell>2013</cell>
<cell>2014</cell>
<cell>2015</cell>
<cell>2016</cell>
<cell>2017</cell>
<cell>2018</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>Population</cell>
<cell>6.957</cell>
<cell>7.041</cell>
<cell>7.126</cell>
<cell>7.211</cell>
<cell>7.296</cell>
<cell>7.380</cell>
<cell>7.464</cell>
<cell>7.548</cell>
<cell>7.631</cell>
</row>
</tabular>

<p>
To study the population from the mathematical point of view, it is convenient to rewrite the data in terms of the independent variable <m>t</m> which stands for the number of years since 2010 and write the population, <m>P</m>, in billions, as a function <m>P=P(t)</m> of years since 2010. Based on the table above, fill in the missing values.
</p>

<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell/>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>7</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>P(t)</m></cell>
<cell>6.957</cell>
<cell>7.041</cell>
<cell/>
<cell>7.211</cell>
<cell>7.296</cell>
<cell>7.380</cell>
<cell>7.464</cell>
<cell/>
<cell>7.631</cell>
</row>
</tabular>


</statement>
<solution>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>7</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>P(t)</m></cell>
<cell>6.957</cell>
<cell>7.041</cell>
<cell>7.126</cell>
<cell>7.211</cell>
<cell>7.296</cell>
<cell>7.380</cell>
<cell>7.464</cell>
<cell>7.548</cell>
<cell>7.631</cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</solution>
</exercise>




<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
A driver of an SUV fills up his gas tank and starts a highway trip to a faraway city. Let <m>G</m>, in gallons, be the amount of gas left in the tank after driving <m>d</m> miles. Of course <m>G</m> is a function of <m>d</m>; that is, <m>G=G(d)</m>. Here is partial data about the function:
</p>

<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>d</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>60</m></cell>
<cell><m>120</m></cell>
<cell><m>180</m></cell>
<cell><m>240</m></cell>
<cell><m>300</m></cell>
<cell><m>360</m></cell>
<cell><m>420</m></cell>
<cell><m>480</m></cell>
<cell><m>510</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>G(d)</m></cell>
<cell><m>17</m></cell>
<cell><m>15</m></cell>
<cell><m>13</m></cell>
<cell><m>11</m></cell>
<cell><m>9</m></cell>
<cell><m>7</m></cell>
<cell/>
<cell/>
<cell/>
<cell/>
</row>
</tabular>

<p>
Find gas mileage of the SUV; that is, the number of miles the SUV gets per gallon. Fill in the missing numbers.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Except for the last column when <m>d=510</m>, note that the miles <m>d</m> go up by <m>60</m> each time, and the gallons <m>G(d)</m> go down by <m>2</m> each time. Using these rules, the completed table is given below.
</p>

<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>d</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>60</m></cell>
<cell><m>120</m></cell>
<cell><m>180</m></cell>
<cell><m>240</m></cell>
<cell><m>300</m></cell>
<cell><m>360</m></cell>
<cell><m>420</m></cell>
<cell><m>480</m></cell>
<cell><m>510</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>G(d)</m></cell>
<cell><m>17</m></cell>
<cell><m>15</m></cell>
<cell><m>13</m></cell>
<cell><m>11</m></cell>
<cell><m>9</m></cell>
<cell><m>7</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>

<p>
Therefore, the car can drive <m>510</m> miles using <m>17</m> gallons of gas. Thus, the gas mileage is
<md>
\frac{510 \text{ miles} }{17 \text{ gallons} } \;=\; 30 \frac{\text{miles} }{\text{gallon} }  .
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




</worksheet>